1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,405
1955.

2
00:00:01,405 --> 00:00:05,614
An American educational film shows a doctor, a small

3
00:00:05,614 --> 00:00:07,637
child, and a vaccination.

4
00:00:08,087 --> 00:00:10,859
For thousands of years, smallpox was one of the

5
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:12,510
deadliest diseases on Earth.

6
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:15,577
On average, about 3 in 10 people who caught it died.

7
00:00:16,027 --> 00:00:19,431
As late as 1967, according to a history of the

8
00:00:19,431 --> 00:00:22,761
eradication campaign, it was still causing an

9
00:00:22,761 --> 00:00:25,722
estimated 10 to 15 million cases a year.

10
00:00:25,722 --> 00:00:29,348
Ten years later, the last natural case was found.

11
00:00:29,348 --> 00:00:31,864
Today, it doesn't exist in nature.

12
00:00:32,564 --> 00:00:35,678
It is the only human infectious disease that has

13
00:00:35,678 --> 00:00:36,975
ever been wiped out.

14
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:40,348
So how do you erase a disease from an entire planet?

15
00:00:40,948 --> 00:00:43,004
This is how the world beat smallpox.

16
00:00:45,604 --> 00:00:46,774
Start with what it was.

17
00:00:47,224 --> 00:00:49,925
Smallpox is caused by the variola virus.

18
00:00:49,925 --> 00:00:53,234
According to the CDC, it has existed for at least

19
00:00:53,234 --> 00:00:54,045
3,000 years.

20
00:00:54,045 --> 00:00:57,894
Smallpox-like rashes have been found on Egyptian mummies.

21
00:00:58,344 --> 00:01:01,484
The earliest written description we know of comes

22
00:01:01,484 --> 00:01:03,471
from China, in the 4th century.

23
00:01:03,471 --> 00:01:06,483
Then it followed trade routes and explorers, to

24
00:01:06,483 --> 00:01:09,559
Japan, to Europe, to Africa, and in the 16th and

25
00:01:09,559 --> 00:01:11,610
17th centuries, to the Americas.

26
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,391
And people noticed something early on.

27
00:01:14,391 --> 00:01:17,396
If you survived smallpox, you were protected from

28
00:01:17,396 --> 00:01:18,500
catching it again.

29
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,982
That observation led to the first weapon against it.

30
00:01:22,432 --> 00:01:23,984
It was called variolation.

31
00:01:23,984 --> 00:01:26,073
Practitioners in Asia developed it.

32
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:29,117
In one method, dried smallpox scabs were blown into

33
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:29,655
the nose.

34
00:01:29,655 --> 00:01:32,759
In Europe and America, it was usually done through a

35
00:01:32,759 --> 00:01:33,953
scratch in the skin.

36
00:01:34,403 --> 00:01:35,208
It was risky.

37
00:01:35,208 --> 00:01:38,242
It meant deliberately exposing people to material

38
00:01:38,242 --> 00:01:39,481
from smallpox sores.

39
00:01:39,481 --> 00:01:41,710
But the numbers were hard to ignore.

40
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:45,745
In Europe, an English aristocrat, Lady Mary Wortley

41
00:01:45,745 --> 00:01:49,190
Montagu, learned of it in Constantinople in 1717,

42
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:52,353
where her husband was the British ambassador.

43
00:01:52,353 --> 00:01:56,008
In 1721, at the urging of Lady Mary and the Princess

44
00:01:56,008 --> 00:01:59,312
of Wales, prisoners and abandoned children were

45
00:01:59,312 --> 00:02:00,085
inoculated.

46
00:02:00,085 --> 00:02:03,389
None of them got smallpox after later exposure.

47
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:07,357
In 1721, during a smallpox epidemic in Boston, the

48
00:02:07,357 --> 00:02:09,961
procedure was first tried in America.

49
00:02:09,961 --> 00:02:13,621
The minister Cotton Mather had learned about it from

50
00:02:13,621 --> 00:02:17,069
an enslaved man in his household, named Onesimus.

51
00:02:17,519 --> 00:02:20,934
About half a century later, it became a question of war.

52
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:24,725
Smallpox had ravaged the American army that invaded

53
00:02:24,725 --> 00:02:26,625
Canada in the winter of 1775.

54
00:02:26,625 --> 00:02:30,031
And fewer than a quarter of Continental soldiers had

55
00:02:30,031 --> 00:02:31,734
caught smallpox naturally.

56
00:02:31,734 --> 00:02:35,009
Most of the British troops they faced were immune.

57
00:02:35,459 --> 00:02:38,690
According to the Military Health System, disease

58
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:41,987
caused an estimated 90 percent of the Continental

59
00:02:41,987 --> 00:02:42,929
Army's deaths.

60
00:02:43,379 --> 00:02:47,847
So, on February 6, 1777, George Washington wrote to

61
00:02:47,847 --> 00:02:50,037
his medical director, Dr.

62
00:02:50,037 --> 00:02:51,439
William Shippen.

63
00:02:51,889 --> 00:02:54,943
I have determined that the troops shall be inoculated.

64
00:02:55,743 --> 00:02:56,701
It was a gamble.

65
00:02:56,701 --> 00:02:59,754
Inoculation could have set off the very outbreak he

66
00:02:59,754 --> 00:03:02,688
feared, and he worried it could hurt recruitment.

67
00:03:02,688 --> 00:03:05,622
Most of it was done in winter, while the fighting

68
00:03:05,622 --> 00:03:08,616
paused, at the camp in Morristown, New Jersey, and

69
00:03:08,616 --> 00:03:09,574
in Philadelphia.

70
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:12,797
The Military Health System calls the strategy

71
00:03:12,797 --> 00:03:13,968
largely successful.

72
00:03:14,768 --> 00:03:16,860
But the real breakthrough came from a cow.

73
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:20,047
The English doctor Edward Jenner had noticed

74
00:03:20,047 --> 00:03:21,726
something about dairymaids.

75
00:03:21,726 --> 00:03:24,587
The ones who had caught cowpox, a disease they

76
00:03:24,587 --> 00:03:27,883
picked up from cows, seemed to be immune to smallpox.

77
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:29,750
In 1796, he tested it.

78
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:32,714
He deliberately gave an 8-year-old boy cowpox.

79
00:03:32,714 --> 00:03:35,033
Then he exposed the boy to smallpox.

80
00:03:35,033 --> 00:03:36,579
The boy didn't get sick.

81
00:03:37,029 --> 00:03:39,790
He repeated the experiment on other children,

82
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:41,140
including his own son.

83
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:44,331
Within twenty years of his findings being published,

84
00:03:44,331 --> 00:03:47,092
his work had been translated into Portuguese,

85
00:03:47,092 --> 00:03:49,301
French, Dutch, Italian and Japanese.

86
00:03:49,751 --> 00:03:53,159
The World Health Organization calls it the first

87
00:03:53,159 --> 00:03:55,572
successful vaccine ever developed.

88
00:03:55,572 --> 00:03:58,057
And Jenner saw where it could lead.

89
00:03:58,057 --> 00:04:01,678
In 1801, he wrote that the annihilation of smallpox

90
00:04:01,678 --> 00:04:03,452
must be the final result.

91
00:04:03,902 --> 00:04:05,342
Armies took notice.

92
00:04:05,342 --> 00:04:08,674
In 1807, Bavaria became the first to require

93
00:04:08,674 --> 00:04:10,795
vaccination of its recruits.

94
00:04:10,795 --> 00:04:14,507
Over the 1800s, the virus in the vaccine changed,

95
00:04:14,507 --> 00:04:18,067
from cowpox to a related virus called vaccinia.

96
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:21,758
In this 1955 film, smallpox vaccine production

97
00:04:21,758 --> 00:04:23,097
starts with calves.

98
00:04:23,097 --> 00:04:26,620
Then the vaccine is processed in a lab, and sealed

99
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:28,170
into tiny glass tubes.

100
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:29,435
It worked.

101
00:04:29,435 --> 00:04:33,587
Smallpox was eliminated from North America in 1952.

102
00:04:33,587 --> 00:04:35,542
Europe followed in 1953.

103
00:04:35,992 --> 00:04:38,808
But in much of the world, smallpox was still everywhere.

104
00:04:39,608 --> 00:04:42,228
So the world tried something that had never been done.

105
00:04:42,678 --> 00:04:46,226
In 1959, the World Health Organization launched a

106
00:04:46,226 --> 00:04:49,700
global plan to eradicate smallpox. It struggled.

107
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:53,320
There wasn't enough money, enough staff, or enough

108
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:53,899
vaccine.

109
00:04:53,899 --> 00:04:56,722
By 1966, smallpox was still widespread.

110
00:04:57,172 --> 00:05:01,218
So in 1967, it started over, with the Intensified

111
00:05:01,218 --> 00:05:02,869
Eradication Program.

112
00:05:02,869 --> 00:05:05,841
Leading it was an American, Dr. D.A.

113
00:05:05,841 --> 00:05:08,979
Henderson, who had trained at the CDC.

114
00:05:09,429 --> 00:05:11,097
The scale was enormous.

115
00:05:11,097 --> 00:05:14,795
At the start, smallpox was endemic in 33 countries,

116
00:05:14,795 --> 00:05:17,696
with a population of 1.2 billion people.

117
00:05:17,696 --> 00:05:21,467
A 2017 history in the Journal of Infectious Diseases

118
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:25,383
puts it at an estimated 10 to 15 million cases a year.

119
00:05:25,383 --> 00:05:28,284
Fewer than 5 percent were ever reported.

120
00:05:28,734 --> 00:05:30,300
They needed better tools.

121
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:32,367
The first was the vaccine itself.

122
00:05:32,367 --> 00:05:35,500
During the program, only heat-stable, freeze-dried

123
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:36,565
vaccine was used.

124
00:05:37,015 --> 00:05:38,668
The second was a tiny tool.

125
00:05:38,668 --> 00:05:40,015
The bifurcated needle.

126
00:05:40,015 --> 00:05:42,893
Two prongs, credited to Benjamin Rubin of Wyeth

127
00:05:42,893 --> 00:05:43,689
Laboratories.

128
00:05:44,139 --> 00:05:47,212
According to the National Library of Medicine, it

129
00:05:47,212 --> 00:05:50,410
needed a quarter of the vaccine used before, almost

130
00:05:50,410 --> 00:05:53,421
every vaccination took, and after sterilization,

131
00:05:53,421 --> 00:05:56,556
each needle could be reused up to a hundred times.

132
00:05:57,356 --> 00:05:59,707
But the biggest change wasn't a tool.

133
00:05:59,707 --> 00:06:00,660
It was an idea.

134
00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:03,655
Vaccinating everyone sounds simple.

135
00:06:03,655 --> 00:06:06,999
In 1967, workers vaccinated 25 million people.

136
00:06:06,999 --> 00:06:11,216
But large parts of the population were still being missed.

137
00:06:11,666 --> 00:06:14,619
A CDC-trained doctor named William Foege pushed a

138
00:06:14,619 --> 00:06:15,764
different approach.

139
00:06:15,764 --> 00:06:17,090
Find every case, fast.

140
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:19,922
Then vaccinate the people around it, in a ring,

141
00:06:19,922 --> 00:06:21,850
before the virus can reach them.

142
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:25,295
It was called surveillance and containment, or ring

143
00:06:25,295 --> 00:06:25,999
vaccination.

144
00:06:25,999 --> 00:06:29,052
And it proved vastly better than indiscriminate mass

145
00:06:29,052 --> 00:06:29,757
vaccination.

146
00:06:30,207 --> 00:06:33,263
Foege also planned an all-out push for the season

147
00:06:33,263 --> 00:06:34,947
when smallpox spread least.

148
00:06:34,947 --> 00:06:37,941
In 1968, it was tested in Sierra Leone, then the

149
00:06:37,941 --> 00:06:40,685
country with the most smallpox in the world.

150
00:06:40,685 --> 00:06:43,554
Within nine months, Sierra Leone reached zero.

151
00:06:44,004 --> 00:06:46,071
It took an army of its own.

152
00:06:46,071 --> 00:06:49,670
More than 150,000 people worked on the program.

153
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:54,111
More than 700 of its field leaders came from 73 countries.

154
00:06:54,111 --> 00:06:57,174
More than 150 of them were from the CDC.

155
00:06:57,974 --> 00:07:01,787
As the goal got close, the team even created its own

156
00:07:01,787 --> 00:07:02,227
honor.

157
00:07:02,227 --> 00:07:04,793
The Order of the Bifurcated Needle.

158
00:07:04,793 --> 00:07:08,532
More than 700 needles were twisted into lapel pins.

159
00:07:09,332 --> 00:07:11,611
Country by country, the map went quiet.

160
00:07:12,061 --> 00:07:16,181
South America in 1971. Asia in 1975.

161
00:07:16,181 --> 00:07:17,897
Africa in 1977.

162
00:07:18,347 --> 00:07:22,296
In late 1975, a 3-year-old girl in Bangladesh became

163
00:07:22,296 --> 00:07:26,168
the last person known to catch the deadlier form of

164
00:07:26,168 --> 00:07:29,053
the disease, variola major, naturally.

165
00:07:29,503 --> 00:07:33,696
And on October 30, 1977, a hospital cook in Merca,

166
00:07:33,696 --> 00:07:38,057
Somalia, was diagnosed with the milder form, variola

167
00:07:38,057 --> 00:07:39,734
minor. He recovered.

168
00:07:39,734 --> 00:07:44,514
He was the last person known to catch smallpox naturally.

169
00:07:44,964 --> 00:07:46,588
There was one more death.

170
00:07:46,588 --> 00:07:49,899
In 1978, a medical photographer at a medical school

171
00:07:49,899 --> 00:07:53,081
in Birmingham, England, caught smallpox and died.

172
00:07:53,081 --> 00:07:56,457
The CDC says her infection was traced to the medical

173
00:07:56,457 --> 00:08:00,093
school building, where smallpox research was being done.

174
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:04,809
Then, on May 8, 1980, the 33rd World Health Assembly

175
00:08:04,809 --> 00:08:07,762
declared the world free of smallpox.

176
00:08:08,362 --> 00:08:09,943
The CDC puts it simply.

177
00:08:09,943 --> 00:08:13,381
Many people consider it the biggest achievement in

178
00:08:13,381 --> 00:08:15,306
international public health.

179
00:08:16,206 --> 00:08:18,119
But the virus itself still exists.

180
00:08:18,569 --> 00:08:21,285
In 1981, four countries still held it:

181
00:08:21,285 --> 00:08:25,073
the United States, England, Russia, and South Africa.

182
00:08:25,073 --> 00:08:28,433
By 1984, England and South Africa had destroyed

183
00:08:28,433 --> 00:08:30,863
their stocks, or transferred them.

184
00:08:30,863 --> 00:08:34,580
Today, the only stocks the World Health Organization

185
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:36,510
oversees are in two places.

186
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:39,869
The CDC in Atlanta, and the VECTOR Institute in

187
00:08:39,869 --> 00:08:41,084
Koltsovo, Russia.

188
00:08:41,534 --> 00:08:44,417
The World Health Organization inspects both

189
00:08:44,417 --> 00:08:47,903
repositories every two years, and keeps an emergency

190
00:08:47,903 --> 00:08:51,255
stockpile of smallpox vaccine, in case the disease

191
00:08:51,255 --> 00:08:52,127
ever returns.

192
00:08:52,577 --> 00:08:56,337
Labs that study the most dangerous viruses work like this.

193
00:08:56,337 --> 00:08:59,579
Full-body suits, connected to a supplied air line.

194
00:08:59,579 --> 00:09:00,875
This one is the U.S.

195
00:09:00,875 --> 00:09:04,247
Army's infectious disease institute at Fort Detrick,

196
00:09:04,247 --> 00:09:04,830
Maryland.

197
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:08,637
Founded in 1969, it's the only Defense Department

198
00:09:08,637 --> 00:09:11,720
lab equipped to safely study highly hazardous

199
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:14,528
viruses at the highest containment level.

200
00:09:14,528 --> 00:09:17,611
Separately, according to the Army, it ran the

201
00:09:17,611 --> 00:09:20,968
pivotal trial that led to FDA approval of another

202
00:09:20,968 --> 00:09:22,133
smallpox vaccine.

203
00:09:22,583 --> 00:09:25,295
After the attacks of September eleventh, and the

204
00:09:25,295 --> 00:09:28,176
anthrax letters, the Pentagon reassessed the threat

205
00:09:28,176 --> 00:09:30,436
that smallpox could be used as a weapon.

206
00:09:30,886 --> 00:09:34,325
Routine smallpox vaccination had already ended.

207
00:09:34,325 --> 00:09:36,959
For American civilians, around 1972.

208
00:09:36,959 --> 00:09:39,227
For military recruits, in 1990.

209
00:09:39,227 --> 00:09:42,886
In December 2002, the Defense Department announced

210
00:09:42,886 --> 00:09:46,251
it would vaccinate designated personnel again,

211
00:09:46,251 --> 00:09:49,910
starting with smallpox response teams and hospital

212
00:09:49,910 --> 00:09:50,495
workers.

213
00:09:50,945 --> 00:09:52,712
A newer vaccine followed.

214
00:09:52,712 --> 00:09:55,184
In 2007, the FDA approved ACAM2000.

215
00:09:55,184 --> 00:09:58,788
It was derived from an older vaccine, Dryvax, which

216
00:09:58,788 --> 00:10:01,048
was grown on the skin of calves.

217
00:10:01,048 --> 00:10:03,662
The new one is grown in cell culture.

218
00:10:04,112 --> 00:10:08,031
The FDA said it's intended for people at high risk

219
00:10:08,031 --> 00:10:09,912
of exposure to smallpox.

220
00:10:09,912 --> 00:10:13,752
At the time, the manufacturer said it had already

221
00:10:13,752 --> 00:10:17,514
supplied 192.5 million doses to the CDC, for the

222
00:10:17,514 --> 00:10:19,787
Strategic National Stockpile.

223
00:10:20,237 --> 00:10:23,354
Vaccination is still part of military life.

224
00:10:23,354 --> 00:10:26,615
This is a seasonal flu vaccine drive, at Camp

225
00:10:26,615 --> 00:10:29,369
Carroll, South Korea, in October 2026.

226
00:10:30,269 --> 00:10:32,437
So, how did the world erase a disease?

227
00:10:32,887 --> 00:10:34,364
Not with one discovery.

228
00:10:34,364 --> 00:10:35,713
With a chain of them.

229
00:10:35,713 --> 00:10:37,318
A dangerous old practice.

230
00:10:37,318 --> 00:10:38,539
A doctor and a cow.

231
00:10:38,539 --> 00:10:40,209
A tiny two-pronged needle.

232
00:10:40,209 --> 00:10:42,264
And an idea about where to look.

233
00:10:42,714 --> 00:10:46,372
And with more than 150,000 people, who went looking

234
00:10:46,372 --> 00:10:49,456
for every last case, until there were none.

235
00:10:49,906 --> 00:10:51,371
Next time on KEPT ALIVE.

236
00:10:51,371 --> 00:10:53,567
Inside the Navy's floating hospital.

237
00:10:54,367 --> 00:10:55,508
For education only.

238
00:10:55,508 --> 00:10:57,128
This is not medical advice.

239
00:10:57,128 --> 00:10:59,950
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
